{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504431,"nonce":"3TyquHzmwpWMlg+i1GJ3aHfPYFI=","target":"56c4728e9c59f4887577a7106c9eba0116bd050a339e7960b56b1d0fa47af9de","message":"**What is wrong.** `ShellBanner` puts `role=\"status\"` on its own root `\u003cdiv\u003e` — `web/src/components/ui/ShellBanner.tsx:53-61`, the attribute at `:55` — and every caller wraps that whole component in a `\u003cShow\u003e`. So the live region and the text it is supposed to announce enter the DOM in the same mutation.\n\nAssistive technology generally announces mutations *inside a live region that already existed*. A region that appears already populated is frequently treated as initial page content and read only if the user happens to be there. NVDA, JAWS and VoiceOver all differ in the details, but \"born with content\" is the case none of them reliably announce. The strip's stated purpose — one polite announcement when it appears — is therefore the case least likely to fire.\n\n**The demonstration.** Two of the three shell strips have this shape:\n\n- `SourceReachBanner`: `\u003cShow when={unreachable().length \u003e 0}\u003e` at `ShellBanner.tsx:135`, wrapping `\u003cShellBanner\u003e` at `:136`. Default `announce`, so `role=\"status\"` is present.\n- `ResyncBanner`: `\u003cShow when={live.detail().resyncReason !== null}\u003e` at `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:125`, wrapping `\u003cShellBanner onDismiss={...}\u003e` at `:126`. Same shape, same role.\n\n`SnoozeBanner` shares the shape but passes `announce={false}` (`ShellBanner.tsx:259`), so it renders no role at all — deliberately, because its rows carry live countdowns (`ShellBanner.tsx:41-46`, and the pin `ShellBanner.test.tsx:226-234`). It is unaffected today but would inherit any fix to the shared component.\n\nThis is **pre-existing, not a regression**. `ResyncBanner` already had this shape before the shared strip existed; the refactor onto `ShellBanner` propagated it rather than introducing it. That is also the argument for fixing it here: one component now owns the behaviour for all three strips, so one change corrects two live regions and settles the policy for the third.\n\nThe app already contains the correct pattern for contrast: `ConnectionBadge` keeps a permanently-mounted `\u003cspan aria-live=\"polite\" aria-atomic=\"true\"\u003e` and swaps only its text (`AppShell.tsx:98-100`), which is why a connection change does announce.\n\n**Why it matters.** These two strips carry the highest-value correctness facts the UI has to state. `SourceReachBanner` is the sole surface for the §B.4 reaper hold (`docs/design/SPEC.md:207`) — its own comment calls that *\"the single highest-value correctness rule in the system and it is completely invisible\"* (`ShellBanner.tsx:94-101`) — and `ResyncBanner` says everything on screen may have been wrong. For a sighted operator the strip is unmissable chrome. For a screen-reader operator it is currently the thing most likely to be silent, which reproduces exactly the \"a calm list reads as calm\" failure the strip was added to prevent.\n\n**The tension, which is why this is not a one-line patch.** The obvious fix — keep an empty `role=\"status\"` mounted always and swap only its inner text — directly contradicts a requirement the repo states twice and pins once.\n\nThe component's own doc comment, `ShellBanner.tsx:13-15`, rule 1: *\"**Silent when there is nothing to say.** Each renders nothing at all on the healthy path, so the table below always starts at the same offset and nothing shifts under a cursor that is already moving.\"* `AppShell.tsx:311-315` repeats it: *\"Each renders nothing at all when it has nothing to say, so the happy path is one flat header and the table below never shifts.\"*\n\nAnd the test asserts it directly. `ShellBanner.test.tsx:116` is titled *\"renders no banner, no live region and nothing to dismiss\"*, and `:124` is `expect(screen.queryAllByRole(\"status\")).toHaveLength(0)` under the comment *\"No strip at all — not an empty one. An empty strip would still cost the header a border and the table below a few pixels of offset.\"* `:140` asserts the same for a merely-degraded source. So the naive fix fails a test that exists on purpose.\n\nA resolution has to satisfy both, and the candidates are genuinely different designs, not variants: a single always-mounted zero-height region owned by the shell that the strips write text into while the visible strip stays conditional; or keeping the region conditional and announcing through a separate mechanism. Either is a decision about where announcement ownership lives, and should be made deliberately rather than in a patch.\n\n**No harness would have caught it.** `web/package.json` has no `playwright` and no `axe` — the devDependencies are vitest, jsdom and `@solidjs/testing-library` (`package.json:27-40`). jsdom has no accessibility tree and no announcement model, so `getAllByRole(\"status\")` passes for a region that is never spoken. Nothing in `npm run verify` (`package.json:19`) can observe this class of defect.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/components/ui/ShellBanner.tsx:53-61` (the role on the conditional root), `:135-136` (`SourceReachBanner`'s `\u003cShow\u003e`), `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:125-126` (`ResyncBanner`'s), `web/src/components/AppShell.tsx:98-100` (the pattern that works), `web/src/components/ui/ShellBanner.test.tsx:116-127` (the requirement that blocks the naive fix).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** A decision is recorded for how a shell strip announces, and implemented once in `ShellBanner`, such that the strip's text is a mutation inside a live region that already existed. The healthy path still renders no visible strip: `ShellBanner.test.tsx:116` and `:129` stay green, or are amended with the recorded reasoning if the chosen design requires a hidden node. `SnoozeBanner`'s `announce={false}` still results in no announcement of its ticking rows.","files":null}]}